              ]
            },
            "title": "Synthesis"
          },
          {
            "text": {
              "status": "generated",
              "div": "<div xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\">Dear colleague, I am writing to inform you about the hospital course of your patient, a 33-year-old male, admitted on 01/03/2013 with acute right lower quadrant abdominal pain, nausea, and low-grade fever. Clinical examination revealed rebound tenderness at McBurney's point. Laboratory findings confirmed leukocytosis, and abdominal ultrasound supported the diagnosis of acute appendicitis. The patient was taken to the operating theatre on the same day, where a laparoscopic appendectomy was performed without intraoperative complications. Histopathology confirmed acute appendicitis. Postoperative recovery was uneventful. The patient received perioperative antibiotics and adequate analgesia. He was mobilised early and tolerated oral intake well. Discharge was on 03/03/2013 in good general condition. Please ensure wound follow-up in approximately 7–10 days. Should any concerns arise, do not hesitate to contact our surgical department.</div>"
            },
            "code": {
